What is Patching?


Patching means applying updates to fix bugs, vulnerabilities, or performance issues in your system or software.
Ye update operating system, software package, ya security component ka ho sakta hai.

Real-World Example: Patching a Linux VM
Imagine you have a Linux VM (Ubuntu) running in AWS or Azure.
This VM is hosting an Apache web server.
Now, a new security vulnerability is reported in Apache that can allow hackers to exploit your system remotely.

Risk if patch is not applied:
Hacker can gain unauthorized access
Data breach or full system compromise ho sakta hai
Compliance violation bhi ho sakti hai (PCI, ISO etc.)

How to Patch the VM:
Check for available patches:
    sudo apt update
Apply the patches:
    sudo apt upgrade
Restart the service (if required):
    sudo systemctl restart apache2
Reboot the VM (only if kernel update applied):
    sudo reboot


Why patching matters?
     Keeps your infrastructure secure
     Protects against zero-day attacks
     Maintains system stability
     Ensures compliance with industry standards



Patching = Regular update lagana taaki koi bhi security gap na rahe.
VM ke andar Apache, Nginx, MySQL jaise tools hote hain — inme bug aate hain, patch fix deta hai.
Tumhara kaam hai timely patch apply karna and ensure karna ki system down na ho.

